window.P107_DATA = {"domains":{"1":"Regulations","2":"Airspace","3":"Weather","4":"Loading and Performance","5":"Operations"},"tests":[{"id":3,"name":"Practice Test 3","figure":{"label":"Figure 3.1","src":"images/f14_3.png","caption":"Fictional Excerpt A. Use this figure for the airspace items that refer to Figure 3.1. Not for navigation.","alt":"Fictional chart with Tamarack Regional inside a dashed blue ring labeled 39, a dashed magenta extension to the west, a faded magenta band, Ashford airport to the east, Hollis private strip, obstacles labeled 2004 (615), 2587 (1022), and 1695 (288), a lake, roads, and numbered points."},"questions":[{"n":1,"stem":"A drone weighs 52 pounds with its battery. The client wants a 4-pound camera mounted for the job. Can the flight be done under Part 107?","options":["Yes, because the drone itself weighs less than 55 pounds","Yes, as long as the camera is mounted before the battery is installed","No, because the takeoff weight would be 56 pounds"],"domain":1,"answer":2,"rationale":"A small UA must weigh less than 55 pounds at takeoff, counting everything on board or attached. 52 + 4 = 56 pounds, which is over the line. A: The empty weight does not decide it. The rule counts payload and attachments at takeoff. B: The order of assembly changes nothing. The total at takeoff is still 56 pounds."},{"n":2,"stem":"A police officer at your job site asks to see your Remote Pilot Certificate. What must you do?","options":["Present it, because local law enforcement officers are among those who may ask","Decline, because only FAA inspectors may ask for it","Offer to email a copy within 10 days"],"domain":1,"answer":0,"rationale":"Section 107.7 requires you to present your certificate and ID on request to the FAA, NTSB, TSA, or any federal, state, or local law enforcement officer. B: The list is wider than the FAA. Local police are on it. C: The certificate must be readily accessible at the site, so a later copy does not meet the rule."},{"n":3,"stem":"Your drone clips a parked car during a job, and the body shop quotes $650 for the repair. What does Part 107 require?","options":["Nothing, because nobody was hurt","A report to the FAA within 10 calendar days","A report to the FAA within 24 hours"],"domain":1,"answer":1,"rationale":"Damage to property other than the drone must be reported within 10 calendar days when the repair cost is more than $500. A: Injuries are only one trigger. Property damage over $500 is another. C: Part 107 gives 10 calendar days, not 24 hours."},{"n":4,"stem":"A client's employee without a Remote Pilot Certificate wants to fly your drone for a few minutes. When is that allowed?","options":["Never, under any circumstances","When the employee is at least 16 years old","When you, as RPIC, directly supervise and can take over immediately"],"domain":1,"answer":2,"rationale":"Section 107.12 lets an uncertificated person manipulate the controls under the direct supervision of a remote PIC who can take direct control at once. A: There is a supervised exception, so \"never\" is too strong. B: Age alone does not allow it. The supervision rule is what matters."},{"n":5,"stem":"A remote pilot knows of a medical condition that could interfere with safe operation. What does Part 107 say?","options":["The pilot must not act as remote PIC or manipulate the controls","The pilot must first obtain a third-class medical certificate","The pilot may fly if a visual observer is present"],"domain":1,"answer":0,"rationale":"Section 107.17 bars anyone from acting as remote PIC, manipulating the controls, or acting as VO if they know or have reason to know of a condition that would interfere with safety. B: Part 107 requires no FAA medical certificate. The rule is self-assessment. C: A VO does not cure a condition that affects the pilot's own ability to fly safely."},{"n":6,"stem":"During an in-flight emergency, how far may you deviate from Part 107?","options":["From any rule for the rest of the day","Only to the extent necessary to meet the emergency","Only from the altitude limit"],"domain":1,"answer":1,"rationale":"Section 107.21 allows deviation from the rules only as far as needed to handle the emergency. A: The permission ends when the emergency is handled. It is not a free pass. C: The rule is not limited to altitude. It covers any rule you must break to deal with the emergency."},{"n":7,"stem":"A farmer asks you to drop small seed packets from your drone over an empty field. What does Part 107 say?","options":["Dropping any object is prohibited","Dropping objects requires a waiver","It is allowed if it does not create an undue hazard to persons or property"],"domain":1,"answer":2,"rationale":"Section 107.23 allows dropping objects as long as it does not create an undue hazard to people or property. A: Part 107 does not ban dropping objects outright. B: No waiver is needed for a drop that creates no undue hazard."},{"n":8,"stem":"You want to fly from a moving boat. Under what condition is this allowed without a waiver?","options":["Over a sparsely populated area, and not carrying another person's property for compensation","Only when a visual observer is on shore","It is never allowed from any moving vehicle"],"domain":1,"answer":0,"rationale":"Section 107.25 allows flight from a moving land or water vehicle only over a sparsely populated area and not while carrying others' property for hire. B: A VO does not create the permission. The location and cargo conditions do. C: The ban is on moving aircraft. Land and water vehicles are allowed under conditions."},{"n":9,"stem":"You finish a glass of wine at 11:00 p.m. Assuming your blood alcohol is below 0.04 and you are not impaired, when is the earliest you may act as remote PIC?","options":["3:00 a.m.","11:00 a.m.","7:00 a.m."],"domain":1,"answer":2,"rationale":"The rule requires 8 hours between drinking and flying, so 11:00 p.m. plus 8 hours is 7:00 a.m. A: Four hours is half the required gap. B: Twelve hours is a sensible personal rule, but the regulation sets 8."},{"n":10,"stem":"You plan to fly during evening civil twilight. What lighting does Part 107 require?","options":["No lighting, because it is not yet night","Anti-collision lighting visible for at least 3 statute miles, with a flash rate sufficient to avoid a collision","Green and red position lights only"],"domain":1,"answer":1,"rationale":"Flight during civil twilight needs the same anti-collision lighting as night flight: visible for at least 3 statute miles with a suitable flash rate. A: Twilight has its own lighting requirement, even though it is not yet night. C: Position lights alone do not meet the rule. It calls for anti-collision lighting."},{"n":11,"stem":"Your visual observer wants to use binoculars to keep the drone in sight. Does that meet the visual line of sight rule?","options":["Yes, any optical aid is acceptable","Yes, if the binoculars are 10x or less","No, the drone must be seen without aids other than corrective lenses"],"domain":1,"answer":2,"rationale":"Visual line of sight means unaided vision, with glasses or contact lenses allowed. Binoculars may be used briefly but cannot be the way you keep the aircraft in sight. A: Optical aids such as binoculars do not satisfy VLOS. B: There is no magnification allowance in the rule."},{"n":12,"stem":"Which task belongs to the visual observer?","options":["Keeping the drone in sight and alerting the remote PIC to hazards","Taking over the controls if the remote PIC is busy","Signing the preflight checklist in place of the remote PIC"],"domain":1,"answer":0,"rationale":"A VO keeps the aircraft in visual line of sight and tells the remote PIC about traffic and hazards. B: Manipulating the controls is not the VO's role. C: Preflight duties belong to the remote PIC."},{"n":13,"stem":"Without a waiver, how many small unmanned aircraft may one person operate at the same time?","options":["Two, if a visual observer watches the second","As many as the control software supports","One"],"domain":1,"answer":2,"rationale":"Section 107.35 limits a person to acting as remote PIC or VO for only one small UA at a time. A: A VO does not raise the limit. It takes a waiver. B: Software capability does not change the rule."},{"n":14,"stem":"A client asks you to carry a small can of gasoline to a remote work site under the drone. What does Part 107 say?","options":["It is allowed under 5 pounds","It is allowed with a waiver","Carrying hazardous material is prohibited, and this rule cannot be waived"],"domain":1,"answer":2,"rationale":"Section 107.36 prohibits carrying hazardous material, and it is not on the list of waivable rules. A: There is no weight allowance for hazardous material. B: This rule is not waivable."},{"n":15,"stem":"A crop-dusting airplane appears low over the field you are working. What does Part 107 require?","options":["The airplane must yield because you were there first","You must yield right of way and give way to the airplane","Whoever is lower has right of way"],"domain":1,"answer":1,"rationale":"Section 107.37 requires a small UA to yield right of way to all aircraft, airborne vehicles, and launch and reentry vehicles. A: Being first gives you no priority. C: Altitude has nothing to do with it. The drone always yields."},{"n":16,"stem":"Your planned flight is in Class C airspace. What do you need before flying?","options":["An authorization from ATC, such as through LAANC or FAADroneZone","Only two-way radio contact with the tower","Nothing, if you stay under 400 feet AGL"],"domain":1,"answer":0,"rationale":"Section 107.41 requires prior ATC authorization for Class B, C, D, and surface Class E airspace. B: Radio contact is not how drone pilots get permission. C: The 400-foot limit does not remove the need for authorization in controlled airspace."},{"n":17,"stem":"Which item is NOT part of the preflight duties in section 107.49?","options":["Assessing the operating environment","Briefing everyone directly participating in the operation","Filing a flight plan with Flight Service"],"domain":1,"answer":2,"rationale":"Section 107.49 covers the environment, crew briefing, control link checks, enough power, and securing objects. A flight plan is not required. A: Assessing the environment is a required step. B: Briefing participants is a required step."},{"n":18,"stem":"Your drone flies at 95 knots through the air into a 15-knot headwind. Does it meet the speed limit?","options":["No, because its airspeed is above 87 knots","Yes, because its groundspeed is 80 knots","No, because the limit is 87 knots of airspeed"],"domain":1,"answer":1,"rationale":"The Part 107 limit is 87 knots of groundspeed. 95 - 15 = 80 knots over the ground, which is within the limit. A: Airspeed is not what the rule measures. C: The rule measures groundspeed, not airspeed."},{"n":19,"stem":"Where is the 3-statute-mile flight visibility in Part 107 measured from?","options":["The control station","The drone","The nearest airport's weather station"],"domain":1,"answer":0,"rationale":"Flight visibility is measured from the control station, where the remote pilot stands. B: The rule is written from the pilot's position, not the aircraft's. C: A reported airport visibility is useful, but the rule measures from the control station."},{"n":20,"stem":"A remote pilot is convicted of a drug offense. What may the FAA do with a pending application?","options":["Deny it for up to 1 year after the conviction","Deny it for life","Nothing, because Part 107 does not address drugs"],"domain":1,"answer":0,"rationale":"Section 107.57 allows the FAA to deny an application for up to 1 year after the date of a final conviction, and to suspend or revoke a certificate. B: The rule sets a period of up to 1 year, not a permanent ban. C: Part 107 does address drug and alcohol offenses."},{"n":21,"stem":"Which of these is an eligibility requirement for a Remote Pilot Certificate?","options":["Holding a valid driver's license","Being able to read, speak, write, and understand English","Owning a registered drone"],"domain":1,"answer":1,"rationale":"Section 107.61 requires the applicant to read, speak, write, and understand English, with exceptions for certain medical reasons. A: No driver's license is needed. C: You do not need to own a drone to hold the certificate."},{"n":22,"stem":"You completed recurrent training on March 10, 2026. Through what date are you current?","options":["March 10, 2028","March 31, 2027","March 31, 2028"],"domain":1,"answer":2,"rationale":"Recency runs 24 calendar months, ending on the last day of the month. From March 2026, that is March 31, 2028. A: Calendar months end on the last day of the month, not on the same date. B: Twelve months is half the required period."},{"n":23,"stem":"A private pilot under Part 61 has a current flight review. Which online course keeps that pilot current for Part 107?","options":["ALC-515","ALC-677","TRUST"],"domain":1,"answer":0,"rationale":"ALC-515 is the recurrent course for Part 61 pilots with a current flight review. B: ALC-677 is the recurrent course for remote pilots who are not using the Part 61 path. C: TRUST is for recreational flyers. It does not serve Part 107."},{"n":24,"stem":"Which rule may the FAA waive?","options":["Section 107.36, hazardous material","Section 107.51, operating limitations","Section 107.27, alcohol and drugs"],"domain":1,"answer":1,"rationale":"Section 107.51 is on the waivable list in section 107.205. A: The hazardous material ban is not waivable. C: The alcohol and drug rules are not waivable."},{"n":25,"stem":"To fly over people under Category 1, the aircraft must","options":["weigh 0.55 pounds or less, including everything on board, with no exposed rotating parts that would lacerate skin","weigh less than 2 pounds and carry propeller guards","carry a Declaration of Compliance label"],"domain":1,"answer":0,"rationale":"Category 1 has no label or declaration. It requires a takeoff weight of 0.55 pounds or less, and no exposed rotating parts that would lacerate skin. B: The weight limit is 0.55 pounds, and guards are not the test. C: A Declaration of Compliance belongs to Categories 2 and 3."},{"n":26,"stem":"Which operation is NOT allowed with a Category 3 aircraft?","options":["Flight over people who are directly participating","Flight over people inside a covered structure","Sustained flight over an open-air assembly"],"domain":1,"answer":2,"rationale":"Category 3 aircraft may not fly over open-air assemblies at all. A: Participants may be overflown in Category 3. B: People under a covered structure that protects them may be overflown."},{"n":27,"stem":"A pilot using a Remote ID broadcast module must","options":["keep the drone within visual line of sight","stay inside an FAA-Recognized Identification Area","fly only at night"],"domain":1,"answer":0,"rationale":"Broadcast module operations must stay within visual line of sight. B: A FRIA is a separate path. The module lets you fly outside one. C: The module has nothing to do with time of day."},{"n":28,"stem":"You registered a drone under Part 107 in June 2025. About when must you renew it?","options":["June 2026","June 2028","Never, because registration lasts for the life of the aircraft"],"domain":1,"answer":1,"rationale":"Part 107 registration is valid for 3 years, so a June 2025 registration runs to about June 2028. A: Registration runs longer than a year. C: Registration expires and must be renewed."},{"n":29,"stem":"Which planned operation needs a waiver?","options":["A flight at 350 feet AGL in Class G airspace","A flight beyond visual line of sight","A flight over your own property"],"domain":1,"answer":1,"rationale":"Operating beyond visual line of sight requires a waiver of section 107.31 under the current rules. A: 350 feet AGL in Class G is within the normal limits. C: Owning the land does not change the rules, but this flight needs no waiver."},{"n":30,"stem":"Class E airspace begins at 700 feet AGL over your site. You will fly at 300 feet AGL. Do you need authorization?","options":["No, because you are in Class G below the Class E floor","Yes, through LAANC","Yes, through a waiver"],"domain":2,"answer":0,"rationale":"Below the 700-foot floor, the airspace is Class G, and Class G needs no authorization. B: LAANC is for controlled airspace, and you are under it. C: A waiver is not the tool for airspace access. And no permission is needed here."},{"n":31,"stem":"A typical Class D area extends from the surface to about","options":["1,200 feet above the airport","10,000 feet MSL","2,500 feet above the airport elevation"],"domain":2,"answer":2,"rationale":"Class D usually reaches about 2,500 feet above the airport, with the exact ceiling printed in the dashed box. A: 1,200 feet is a common Class E floor. B: 10,000 feet MSL is a typical Class B ceiling."},{"n":32,"stem":"At what altitude does Class A airspace begin?","options":["18,000 feet MSL","14,500 feet MSL","10,000 feet MSL"],"domain":2,"answer":0,"rationale":"Class A begins at 18,000 feet MSL. B: 14,500 feet MSL is where Class E begins in areas with no other floor. C: 10,000 feet MSL is a typical Class B ceiling."},{"n":33,"stem":"Your site lies inside a restricted area. Whose permission do you need?","options":["The local police department","The using or controlling agency for that area","No one, if you stay below 400 feet AGL"],"domain":2,"answer":1,"rationale":"Section 107.45 requires permission from the using or controlling agency, which the chart's special use airspace table names. A: Local police do not control restricted airspace. C: Restricted areas often start at the surface, so altitude does not exempt you."},{"n":34,"stem":"What does a National Security Area mean for a drone pilot?","options":["It is always prohibited","It is a training area for military jets","Pilots are asked to avoid it voluntarily, and it may be temporarily prohibited by NOTAM"],"domain":2,"answer":2,"rationale":"A National Security Area asks for voluntary avoidance. A NOTAM can make it temporarily prohibited. A: Only a prohibited area is always off-limits. B: That describes a Military Operations Area."},{"n":35,"stem":"A military training route on the chart is labeled IR 1207. What does the four-digit number tell you?","options":["The route is flown at or below 1,500 feet AGL","The route is flown above 1,500 feet AGL","The route is closed to drones"],"domain":2,"answer":0,"rationale":"Four-digit route numbers mean the route has no segment above 1,500 feet AGL. Three digits mean it goes above 1,500 feet. B: That is the meaning of a three-digit number. C: A route is not closed to drones, but you must watch for fast, low traffic."},{"n":36,"stem":"A stadium with 30,000 or more seats has a game today. What is the standard flight restriction?","options":["Within 1 nautical mile and below 1,000 feet AGL, during the game only","Within 5 nautical miles at all altitudes, all day","Within 3 nautical miles and below 3,000 feet AGL, from 1 hour before to 1 hour after the event"],"domain":2,"answer":2,"rationale":"The standing restriction for large stadium events covers 3 nautical miles and up to 3,000 feet AGL, from 1 hour before the scheduled start to 1 hour after the end. A: Both numbers are too small, and the time window is wrong. B: This overstates the size and the time window."},{"n":37,"stem":"(Refer to Figure 3.1.) You will fly at 300 feet AGL over Hollis (Pvt), northwest of Tamarack. What airspace are you in?","options":["Class D, so you need authorization","Class E to the surface, so you need authorization","Class G, so no authorization is needed"],"domain":2,"fig":true,"answer":2,"rationale":"Hollis lies inside the faded magenta band but outside the dashed lines. Class E begins at 700 feet AGL there, so at 300 feet you are in Class G. A: Hollis is outside the dashed blue Class D ring. B: The dashed magenta surface area is west of Tamarack, not at Hollis."},{"n":38,"stem":"(Refer to Figure 3.1.) What is the ground elevation at the obstacle labeled 1695 (288), south of Tamarack?","options":["1,407 feet MSL","1,983 feet MSL","288 feet MSL"],"domain":2,"fig":true,"answer":0,"rationale":"The bold number is the top in feet MSL and the parentheses give the height AGL. 1,695 - 288 = 1,407 feet MSL. B: This adds the numbers instead of subtracting them. C: 288 is the height of the obstacle above the ground, not the elevation of the ground."},{"n":39,"stem":"(Refer to Figure 3.1.) You inspect the obstacle labeled 1695 (288) and stay within 400 feet of it. What is your highest allowed altitude?","options":["1,807 feet MSL","2,095 feet MSL","688 feet MSL"],"domain":2,"fig":true,"answer":1,"rationale":"The structure allowance is 400 feet above the top: 288 + 400 = 688 feet AGL. With the ground at 1,407 feet MSL, that is 1,407 + 688 = 2,095 feet MSL. A: This adds 400 feet to the ground elevation and ignores the structure allowance. C: 688 is the right answer in feet AGL, but it is not an MSL altitude."},{"n":40,"stem":"(Refer to Figure 3.1.) Which frequency would you monitor to hear traffic at Ashford, east of Tamarack?","options":["118.3","122.95","122.8"],"domain":2,"fig":true,"answer":2,"rationale":"Ashford's data block shows 122.8 followed by C, which marks it as the CTAF. A: 118.3 is Tamarack's tower frequency. B: 122.95 is Tamarack's UNICOM."},{"n":41,"stem":"(Refer to Figure 3.1.) What is the length of the longest runway at Ashford?","options":["3,800 feet","1,188 feet","380 feet"],"domain":2,"fig":true,"answer":0,"rationale":"The last number on the second line, 38, is the longest runway in hundreds of feet: 3,800 feet. B: 1,188 is Ashford's field elevation in feet MSL. C: Runway length is in hundreds of feet, so 38 means 3,800."},{"n":42,"stem":"A METAR reports BKN008. To meet the Part 107 cloud clearance rule, what is the highest you may fly?","options":["800 feet AGL","400 feet AGL","300 feet AGL"],"domain":3,"answer":2,"rationale":"BKN008 puts the cloud base at 800 feet AGL, and you must stay 500 feet below clouds: 800 - 500 = 300 feet AGL. A: Flying at the cloud base breaks the 500-foot rule. B: 400 feet would leave only 400 feet of clearance."},{"n":43,"stem":"The temperature is 72°F and the dew point is 63°F. About where will cumulus clouds form?","options":["About 900 feet AGL","About 2,000 feet AGL","About 6,300 feet AGL"],"domain":3,"answer":1,"rationale":"Divide the spread by 4.4 and multiply by 1,000: 9 / 4.4 × 1,000 ≈ 2,000 feet AGL. A: This uses the spread directly as hundreds of feet. C: This misreads the dew point as the cloud base."},{"n":44,"stem":"Which conditions favor radiation fog?","options":["A clear night, light wind, and a small temperature and dew point spread","A strong wind blowing warm air over a cold sea","A fast-moving cold front with heavy rain"],"domain":3,"answer":0,"rationale":"Radiation fog forms when the ground cools on clear, calm nights and the air near it reaches its dew point. B: Moist air moving over a colder surface makes advection fog. C: A front with rain does not produce radiation fog."},{"n":45,"stem":"An 18-pound drone makes a level turn at a 45-degree bank. About what load does the airframe carry?","options":["18 pounds","25 pounds","36 pounds"],"domain":4,"answer":1,"rationale":"The load factor at 45 degrees is about 1.41, so 18 × 1.41 ≈ 25 pounds. A: A level banked turn adds load. The weight alone is too low. C: A doubled load belongs to a 60-degree bank."},{"n":46,"stem":"A teammate says, \"We don't need the checklist. Rules are for beginners.\" Which antidote fits this attitude?","options":["\"Not so fast. Think first.\"","\"It could happen to me.\"","\"Follow the rules. They are usually right.\""],"domain":5,"answer":2,"rationale":"Brushing off rules is anti-authority, and its antidote is \"Follow the rules. They are usually right.\" A: That is the antidote for impulsivity. B: That is the antidote for invulnerability."},{"n":47,"stem":"In the IMSAFE checklist, what does the S stand for?","options":["Sleep","Stress","Safety"],"domain":5,"answer":1,"rationale":"IMSAFE stands for Illness, Medication, Stress, Alcohol, Fatigue, and Emotion. A: Sleep is covered under Fatigue. C: Safety is the goal, not one of the letters."},{"n":48,"stem":"Gusty winds and a nearby power line fall under which PAVE letter?","options":["V, for enVironment","P, for Pilot","E, for External pressures"],"domain":5,"answer":0,"rationale":"The V in PAVE covers the environment, including weather, terrain, obstacles, and airspace. B: P covers the pilot's own fitness. C: E covers pressures such as clients, deadlines, and money."},{"n":49,"stem":"At night, what is the best way to spot a dim object?","options":["Stare straight at where you think it is","Look a little to one side of it","Close one eye"],"domain":5,"answer":1,"rationale":"The center of the eye sees poorly in dim light, so off-center viewing works better at night. A: The center of vision has a night blind spot. C: Closing one eye does not help night vision."},{"n":50,"stem":"You have worked outside for hours in the heat and feel a headache and fatigue. What is the likely cause, and what should you do?","options":["Hyperventilation, so breathe faster","Hypoxia, so move to a lower elevation","Dehydration, so stop, drink water, and get into shade"],"domain":5,"answer":2,"rationale":"Headache and tiredness in the heat are early signs of dehydration. Drink before you feel thirsty and take a break. A: Breathing faster would make hyperventilation worse, and the symptoms point to heat. B: Hypoxia is a high-altitude problem for crewed pilots, not a ground-level one."},{"n":51,"stem":"Before flying near tall trees, how should you set the return-to-home altitude?","options":["As low as possible to save battery","Higher than the tallest obstacle on the return path","At exactly 400 feet MSL"],"domain":5,"answer":1,"rationale":"Return-to-home flies a straight path at a set height, so that height must clear everything between the drone and home. A: A low setting can fly the drone into the trees. C: MSL is the wrong reference, and a fixed number ignores the actual obstacles."},{"n":52,"stem":"Your drone flies away and heads toward a nearby towered airport. What should you do?","options":["Wait until the battery runs out, then report within 10 days","Call the airport's control tower right away to warn them","Turn off the controller"],"domain":5,"answer":1,"rationale":"When a flyaway may reach an airport or controlled airspace, warn ATC at once so it can protect other traffic. A: Waiting leaves crewed aircraft at risk. C: Turning off the controller usually removes your last chance to regain control."},{"n":53,"stem":"A lithium battery is swollen after a flight. What should you do?","options":["Stop using it and dispose of it according to the manufacturer and local rules","Charge it slowly to reduce the swelling","Use it only for short flights"],"domain":5,"answer":0,"rationale":"A swollen pack is damaged and can catch fire. Take it out of service and dispose of it properly. B: Charging a damaged pack raises the fire risk. C: There is no safe short flight with a swollen pack."},{"n":54,"stem":"A lithium battery is burning. What is most effective at cooling the pack to keep the fire from spreading to other cells?","options":["Covering it with a plastic bag","A Halon extinguisher","Large amounts of water"],"domain":5,"answer":2,"rationale":"Water cools the cells, which slows thermal runaway. A: Plastic melts and does nothing to cool the pack. B: Halon knocks the flames down but does not stop the cells from heating each other."},{"n":55,"stem":"How is the number 9 spoken on aviation radio?","options":["Nine","Nein","Niner"],"domain":5,"answer":2,"rationale":"Pilots say \"niner\" so that 9 is not confused with other words. A: Plain \"nine\" is avoided on the radio. B: This is not aviation phraseology."},{"n":56,"stem":"Unless an airport shows otherwise, which way do turns in the traffic pattern go?","options":["Left","Right","Either way, at the pilot's choice"],"domain":5,"answer":0,"rationale":"The standard traffic pattern uses left turns. Right traffic is marked on charts and at the field. B: Right turns apply only when published. C: Pilots follow the published pattern, not personal choice."},{"n":57,"stem":"An airport's rotating beacon flashes white and yellow. What kind of airport is it?","options":["A civilian land airport","A water airport","A military airport"],"domain":5,"answer":1,"rationale":"White and yellow mark a water airport. A: A civilian land airport flashes white and green. C: A military airport shows two quick white flashes between green flashes."},{"n":58,"stem":"At a segmented circle, what do the traffic pattern indicators show?","options":["The wind speed","The direction of turns in the traffic pattern","The runway length"],"domain":5,"answer":1,"rationale":"Traffic pattern indicators show which way aircraft turn to and from the runway. A: Wind is shown by the wind cone. C: Runway length is on the chart and in the Chart Supplement."},{"n":59,"stem":"Which is an example of good crew resource management with a visual observer?","options":["The VO repeats the warning back, and the remote PIC confirms the action taken","The VO stays silent to avoid distracting the pilot","The remote PIC hands the controls to the VO during busy moments"],"domain":5,"answer":0,"rationale":"Closed-loop communication, in which the message is repeated and confirmed, is a core CRM skill. B: A silent VO defeats the purpose of having one. C: Handing controls to an uncertificated VO is not how the roles work."},{"n":60,"stem":"A firmware update installs on the morning of a client job. What is the best practice?","options":["Fly the job at once, since updates fix problems","Test the drone in a safe, open area before flying the job","Uninstall the update without checking"],"domain":5,"answer":1,"rationale":"A new update can change behavior, so test in a safe place before you rely on it. A: Trusting an untested update on a job removes your margin. C: Rolling back blindly can cause its own problems. Test first."}]},{"id":4,"name":"Practice Test 4","figure":{"label":"Figure 4.1","src":"images/f14_4.png","caption":"Fictional Excerpt B. Use this figure for the airspace items that refer to Figure 4.1. Not for navigation.","alt":"Fictional chart with Port Ellery International in two solid magenta rings labeled 48 over SFC and 48 over 22, restricted area R-4411, Cedar MOA, Seaview airport, a bay, obstacles labeled 1320 (640) and 1038 (402), and numbered points."},"questions":[{"n":1,"stem":"Which is part of a small unmanned aircraft system?","options":["The control station and the communication links that control the aircraft","The vehicle that carries the pilot to the site","The airspace the drone flies in"],"domain":1,"answer":0,"rationale":"A small UAS is the aircraft plus its associated parts, such as the control station and command and control links. B: Your car is not part of the system. C: Airspace is where the system operates, not part of it."},{"n":2,"stem":"You registered as a recreational flyer and received one number for all your drones. Can you use that number for paid Part 107 work?","options":["Yes, a drone registration covers any kind of flying","No, each drone flown under Part 107 needs its own Part 107 registration","Yes, if you add \"107\" to the number"],"domain":1,"answer":1,"rationale":"Part 107 registration is per aircraft, and recreational registrations do not transfer to Part 107. A: The two registration types are separate. C: There is no way to convert a number by editing it."},{"n":3,"stem":"During preflight, the controller shows a weak control link warning that you cannot clear. What does Part 107 require?","options":["Fly anyway, but stay close","Ask a visual observer to watch more closely","Do not fly until the system is in a condition for safe operation"],"domain":1,"answer":2,"rationale":"Section 107.15 requires the remote PIC to check that the small UAS is in a condition for safe operation before flight. A: Staying close does not fix an unsafe system. B: A VO cannot repair a faulty link."},{"n":4,"stem":"A client insists that you fly in conditions you judge unsafe. Who has final authority over the flight?","options":["The client, because the client is paying","The person manipulating the controls, if different from the remote PIC","The remote PIC"],"domain":1,"answer":2,"rationale":"The remote PIC is directly responsible for, and is the final authority on, the operation. A: Paying for the job gives no authority over safety. B: Final authority rests with the remote PIC, even when someone else is on the sticks."},{"n":5,"stem":"To impress a client, a pilot makes low, fast passes over a crowd of spectators. Which rule is this most likely to break?","options":["The rule against careless or reckless operation","The registration rule","The recurrent training rule"],"domain":1,"answer":0,"rationale":"Section 107.23 prohibits operating in a careless or reckless manner that endangers life or property. B: Registration has nothing to do with how you fly. C: Recency is unrelated to this behavior."},{"n":6,"stem":"Without a waiver, may you operate a small UA from a moving airplane?","options":["Yes, if the airplane is below 400 feet","Yes, if a visual observer is aboard","No"],"domain":1,"answer":2,"rationale":"Section 107.25 prohibits operation from a moving aircraft. A: Altitude does not create an exception. B: A VO does not change the rule."},{"n":7,"stem":"You stopped drinking 10 hours ago, but you still feel the effects. May you act as remote PIC?","options":["No, because you may not fly while under the influence of alcohol","Yes, because more than 8 hours have passed","Yes, if a visual observer is present"],"domain":1,"answer":0,"rationale":"The 8-hour rule is only one part. You also may not fly while under the influence, however long ago you drank. B: Passing 8 hours does not cancel the under-the-influence rule. C: A VO does not make an impaired pilot legal."},{"n":8,"stem":"A remote pilot earned the certificate in 2019 and has not taken any training since April 6, 2021. What is needed before flying at night?","options":["Nothing, because the certificate does not expire","Completing the current recurrent training, which includes the night material","A night waiver from FAADroneZone"],"domain":1,"answer":1,"rationale":"Night operations require a pilot to have passed the initial test or completed recurrent training after April 6, 2021. A: Holding the certificate is not enough for night flight, and this pilot is not current either. C: Night flight no longer needs a waiver if the training and lighting rules are met."},{"n":9,"stem":"You fly without a visual observer. Who must be able to see the drone at all times?","options":["The client","Anyone at the site","The remote PIC and the person manipulating the controls"],"domain":1,"answer":2,"rationale":"Without a VO, the remote PIC and the person manipulating the controls must keep the aircraft in visual line of sight. A: The client has no role in the VLOS rule. B: The rule names specific people, not anyone at the site."},{"n":10,"stem":"What does Part 107 require between the remote PIC, the person manipulating the controls, and the visual observer?","options":["A written flight plan","Effective communication with each other at all times","That all three hold Remote Pilot Certificates"],"domain":1,"answer":1,"rationale":"Section 107.33 requires the crew to keep effective communication at all times. A: No written plan is required. C: A VO does not need a certificate."},{"n":11,"stem":"How close may you fly to a crewed aircraft?","options":["No closer than 500 feet","As close as needed, if you have right of way","Not so close as to create a collision hazard"],"domain":1,"answer":2,"rationale":"Section 107.37 prohibits operating so close to another aircraft that it creates a collision hazard. A: The rule sets no fixed distance. B: A drone never has right of way over crewed aircraft."},{"n":12,"stem":"What must a Category 2 aircraft have?","options":["A Declaration of Compliance accepted by the FAA, and a label showing it is eligible for Category 2","An airworthiness certificate","A weight of 0.55 pounds or less"],"domain":1,"answer":0,"rationale":"Category 2 requires an FAA-accepted Declaration of Compliance and a label, plus the injury and rotating-part limits. B: An airworthiness certificate belongs to Category 4. C: The 0.55-pound limit belongs to Category 1."},{"n":13,"stem":"Which category of operations over people requires an airworthiness certificate?","options":["Category 2","Category 3","Category 4"],"domain":1,"answer":2,"rationale":"Category 4 aircraft must have an airworthiness certificate issued under Part 21. A: Category 2 uses a Declaration of Compliance. B: Category 3 also uses a Declaration of Compliance."},{"n":14,"stem":"Inside a closed or restricted-access site, Category 3 flight over people is allowed when","options":["everyone inside has been notified that a small UA may fly over them","the drone weighs less than 2 pounds","the remote PIC has a waiver"],"domain":1,"answer":0,"rationale":"Category 3 can fly over people inside a closed or restricted-access site if everyone there has been told the drone may fly over them. B: Category 3 is defined by injury limits, not a 2-pound rule. C: No waiver is needed when the category conditions are met."},{"n":15,"stem":"Near an airport, what does section 107.43 require?","options":["Staying at least 5 miles from every airport","Calling the airport manager before every flight","Not interfering with operations and traffic patterns at any airport, heliport, or seaplane base"],"domain":1,"answer":2,"rationale":"Section 107.43 prohibits interfering with operations and traffic patterns at airports, heliports, and seaplane bases. A: Part 107 sets no fixed distance. B: There is no general requirement to call the manager."},{"n":16,"stem":"May a Part 107 drone transmit ADS-B Out?","options":["Yes, it is required in controlled airspace","Yes, at any time","No, unless otherwise authorized by the FAA"],"domain":1,"answer":2,"rationale":"Section 107.53 prohibits ADS-B Out on a small UA unless the FAA authorizes it. A: ADS-B Out is not required for drones. B: The default is that it is not allowed."},{"n":17,"stem":"A remote pilot refuses a law enforcement officer's request to take an alcohol test. What may follow?","options":["Nothing, because Part 107 does not address this","Denial of an application for up to 1 year, or suspension or revocation of the certificate","A fine only"],"domain":1,"answer":1,"rationale":"Section 107.59 treats a refusal as grounds for denial for up to 1 year, or suspension or revocation. A: Part 107 does address it. C: The certificate itself can be affected."},{"n":18,"stem":"Your application has passed the TSA check. How do you get your temporary certificate?","options":["Print it from IACRA, following the confirmation email","Wait for it in the mail","Pick it up at the testing center"],"domain":1,"answer":0,"rationale":"After the security check, you receive an email with instructions to print the temporary certificate from IACRA. The plastic card comes by mail later. B: Only the permanent card arrives by mail. C: The testing center does not issue certificates."},{"n":19,"stem":"Your certificate was issued 5 years ago and you have never taken recurrent training. Which is true?","options":["The certificate has expired, and you must start over","The certificate is still valid, but you may not exercise its privileges until you complete recurrent training","You must retake the knowledge test at PSI"],"domain":1,"answer":1,"rationale":"The certificate does not expire, but recency lapses after 24 calendar months. The free online recurrent training restores it. A: There is no expiration date on the certificate. C: Recurrent training is online and free. No retest at PSI is required."},{"n":20,"stem":"A company asks the FAA for a waiver covering several Part 107 sections. Which section can the FAA not waive?","options":["Section 107.41, operation in certain airspace","Section 107.9, accident reporting","Section 107.33, visual observer"],"domain":1,"answer":1,"rationale":"Accident reporting is not on the list of waivable rules in section 107.205. A: The airspace rule is waivable. C: The visual observer rule is waivable."},{"n":21,"stem":"Which information does a standard Remote ID drone broadcast that a broadcast module does not?","options":["The location of the control station","The aircraft's serial number","The aircraft's velocity"],"domain":1,"answer":0,"rationale":"Standard Remote ID includes the control station's location. A module broadcasts the takeoff location instead. B: Both broadcast an identifier. C: Both broadcast the aircraft's velocity."},{"n":22,"stem":"You fly a drone with no Remote ID equipment at an FAA-Recognized Identification Area. Where must you and the drone be?","options":["Anywhere within 5 miles of the FRIA","Inside the FRIA, but the drone may go beyond visual line of sight","Inside the FRIA's boundary, with the drone in visual line of sight"],"domain":1,"answer":2,"rationale":"Flying without Remote ID at a FRIA requires the pilot and the aircraft to stay inside its boundary, within visual line of sight. A: There is no 5-mile allowance. B: VLOS still applies at a FRIA."},{"n":23,"stem":"You own three drones for Part 107 work. How many registrations do you need?","options":["One for all three","Three, one for each drone","None, if each weighs under 0.55 pounds"],"domain":1,"answer":1,"rationale":"Under Part 107, each aircraft is registered separately. A: One number for all aircraft is a recreational feature. C: Part 107 has no weight exemption from registration."},{"n":24,"stem":"How do you file a Part 107 accident report with the FAA?","options":["Through FAADroneZone","By calling the local police","By mailing the AKTR to the FAA"],"domain":1,"answer":0,"rationale":"Accident reports are filed through FAADroneZone. B: Police are not the FAA's reporting channel. C: The AKTR is your test report, not an accident form."},{"n":25,"stem":"What must the remote PIC ensure in case control of the drone is lost?","options":["That the drone will pose no undue hazard to other people, aircraft, or property","That the drone has insurance","That the drone can fly for 30 minutes"],"domain":1,"answer":0,"rationale":"Section 107.19 requires the remote PIC to ensure the aircraft poses no undue hazard if control is lost. B: Part 107 does not require insurance. C: There is no endurance requirement."},{"n":26,"stem":"Who may act as a visual observer?","options":["Only a certificated remote pilot","Only a person 18 or older","Any person, without a certificate"],"domain":1,"answer":2,"rationale":"A VO needs no certificate or minimum age, but must be able to do the job and communicate with the crew. A: The VO does not need a Remote Pilot Certificate. B: Part 107 sets no minimum age for VOs."},{"n":27,"stem":"When operating under Part 107, what must you have with you?","options":["Your Remote Pilot Certificate and identification, readily accessible","Your AKTR and your passport","Only the aircraft's registration certificate"],"domain":1,"answer":0,"rationale":"Section 107.7 requires the certificate and ID to be in your possession and readily accessible. B: The AKTR is not a certificate, and a passport is not specifically required. C: The rule centers on the pilot's certificate and ID."},{"n":28,"stem":"A 15-year-old wants to be remote PIC for a paid job. Is this allowed?","options":["Yes, if a parent is present","No, because a person must be at least 16 to hold a Remote Pilot Certificate","Yes, if the drone weighs under 2 pounds"],"domain":1,"answer":1,"rationale":"A certificate holder must be 16 or older, and the remote PIC must hold one. A 15-year-old may only fly under supervision. A: A parent does not satisfy the certificate requirement. C: Weight has no bearing on who may be remote PIC."},{"n":29,"stem":"You will be paid to take photos of a house for a real estate listing. Which rules apply?","options":["The recreational exception","No rules, if the house is yours","Part 107"],"domain":1,"answer":2,"rationale":"Flying for compensation or in support of a business falls under Part 107. A: The recreational exception covers flying purely for fun. B: Ownership does not remove the rules for commercial work."},{"n":30,"stem":"A typical Class C outer shelf begins at about","options":["1,200 feet above the airport and extends to about 10 nautical miles","the surface and extends to about 20 nautical miles","5,000 feet MSL"],"domain":2,"answer":0,"rationale":"The outer shelf of a typical Class C runs from about 1,200 feet above the airport out to about 10 nautical miles. B: The surface portion is the inner core, which is about 5 nautical miles. C: The shelf floor is not a fixed MSL figure like this."},{"n":31,"stem":"A typical Class B ceiling is about","options":["2,500 feet above the airport","10,000 feet MSL","18,000 feet MSL"],"domain":2,"answer":1,"rationale":"Class B usually reaches up to about 10,000 feet MSL. A: That is a typical Class D ceiling. C: 18,000 feet MSL is where Class A begins."},{"n":32,"stem":"A faded blue band on a sectional means","options":["Class E begins at 700 feet AGL","Class G extends up to 14,500 feet MSL","Class E begins at 1,200 feet AGL"],"domain":2,"answer":2,"rationale":"Faded blue marks the edge of Class E with a floor of 1,200 feet AGL. Faded magenta marks a 700-foot floor. A: That is the faded magenta band. B: The 14,500-foot floor applies where nothing else is shown."},{"n":33,"stem":"Where no other floor is shown, Class E begins at","options":["14,500 feet MSL","18,000 feet MSL","1,200 feet AGL"],"domain":2,"answer":0,"rationale":"In areas without another floor, Class E begins at 14,500 feet MSL. B: 18,000 feet MSL is the start of Class A. C: 1,200 feet AGL is marked by the faded blue band."},{"n":34,"stem":"What does an alert area on a sectional mean?","options":["Drones are prohibited there","There may be a high volume of pilot training or unusual aerial activity, so be alert","You must get permission from the using agency"],"domain":2,"answer":1,"rationale":"An alert area warns of heavy training or unusual activity. No permission is needed, but extra caution is. A: Alert areas do not prohibit flight. C: Permission is required for restricted areas, not alert areas."},{"n":35,"stem":"What is a warning area?","options":["An area extending from 3 nautical miles outward from the coast, with activity that may be hazardous","An area over a city where drones are banned","A TFR around a stadium"],"domain":2,"answer":0,"rationale":"A warning area lies over international waters beyond 3 nautical miles from the coast and contains activity that may be hazardous. B: No special use airspace is defined that way. C: Stadium restrictions are TFRs, not warning areas."},{"n":36,"stem":"On a UAS Facility Map, a grid square shows 0. What does that mean?","options":["You may fly up to 400 feet without permission","The area is closed to all drones","LAANC cannot give an automatic approval there, so further coordination through FAADroneZone is needed"],"domain":2,"answer":2,"rationale":"A zero means no automatic approval at any altitude. You can still request authorization through FAADroneZone. A: Zero does not mean unrestricted. B: The grid does not close the area. It sets the automatic approval limit."},{"n":37,"stem":"(Refer to Figure 4.1.) Which frequency would you listen to for recorded airport information at Port Ellery International?","options":["119.7","127.05","122.95"],"domain":2,"fig":true,"answer":1,"rationale":"ATIS, the recorded airport information broadcast, is listed as 127.05. A: 119.7 is the control tower frequency. C: 122.95 is the UNICOM."},{"n":38,"stem":"(Refer to Figure 4.1.) How high does the Class C inner ring reach above Port Ellery International?","options":["4,800 feet","3,958 feet","2,200 feet"],"domain":2,"fig":true,"answer":1,"rationale":"The ceiling is 4,800 feet MSL and the field elevation is 842 feet: 4,800 - 842 = 3,958 feet above the airport. A: 4,800 feet is the ceiling in MSL, not its height above the airport. C: 2,200 feet MSL is the outer ring's floor."},{"n":39,"stem":"(Refer to Figure 4.1.) What is the ground elevation at the obstacle labeled 1320 (640)?","options":["680 feet MSL","1,960 feet MSL","640 feet MSL"],"domain":2,"fig":true,"answer":0,"rationale":"Subtract the height AGL from the top MSL: 1,320 - 640 = 680 feet MSL. B: This adds the two numbers. C: 640 is the obstacle's height above the ground."},{"n":40,"stem":"(Refer to Figure 4.1.) You fly at 400 feet AGL beside the obstacle labeled 1038 (402), west of the airport, between the rings. Are you in Class C?","options":["Yes, because you are inside the outer ring","Yes, because Class C starts at the surface everywhere inside both rings","No, because the Class C floor there is 2,200 feet MSL, well above you"],"domain":2,"fig":true,"answer":2,"rationale":"The ground there is 1,038 - 402 = 636 feet MSL, so you are at about 1,036 feet MSL. The outer ring's floor is 2,200 feet MSL. A: Being inside a ring does not put you in Class C until you reach its floor. B: Only the inner ring starts at the surface."},{"n":41,"stem":"(Refer to Figure 4.1.) Which statement about Seaview (S17) is true?","options":["It has a control tower on 122.9","Its field elevation is 2,000 feet","It has no tower, and its CTAF is 122.9"],"domain":2,"fig":true,"answer":2,"rationale":"Seaview is magenta with \"122.9 C,\" which means no tower and a CTAF of 122.9. A: Magenta data and the C show it is non-towered. B: Its elevation is 208 feet. The 20 is the runway length, 2,000 feet."},{"n":42,"stem":"A METAR shows VV004. What does this mean?","options":["Visibility of 4 statute miles","Variable wind at 4 knots","The sky is obscured, with a vertical visibility of 400 feet"],"domain":3,"answer":2,"rationale":"VV reports vertical visibility into an obscured sky, here 400 feet, and it counts as a ceiling. A: Visibility is reported in SM, not with VV. B: Variable wind is reported as VRB."},{"n":43,"stem":"A TAF includes \"PROB30 1416/1420 TSRA.\" What does PROB30 mean?","options":["A 30 percent chance of thunderstorms with rain between 1600Z and 2000Z on the 14th","Thunderstorms for 30 minutes","Thunderstorms at 3,000 feet"],"domain":3,"answer":0,"rationale":"PROB30 gives a 30 percent probability for the conditions in the stated time window. B: The number is a probability, not a duration. C: It has nothing to do with altitude."},{"n":44,"stem":"Which conditions are typical of stable air?","options":["Gusty winds and towering clouds","Smooth air, layered clouds, and often poor visibility in haze","Heavy showers and good visibility"],"domain":3,"answer":1,"rationale":"Stable air resists rising, so it brings smooth air, stratus clouds, and haze that can trap poor visibility. A: Gusts and towering clouds are signs of unstable air. C: Showers and good visibility go with unstable air."},{"n":45,"stem":"A multirotor's center of gravity sits well forward of center. What is the usual effect?","options":["The front motors work harder, which reduces endurance and control margin","Nothing, because flight controllers cancel any imbalance for free","The drone flies faster with no penalty"],"domain":4,"answer":0,"rationale":"The motors on the heavy side must work harder to hold the aircraft level, which costs battery and control reserve. B: The controller compensates, but it uses power and margin to do it. C: An off-center load has a cost, not a free gain."},{"n":46,"stem":"A teammate shrugs and says, \"Whatever happens, happens. The wind will do what it wants.\" Which hazardous attitude is this?","options":["Macho","Anti-authority","Resignation"],"domain":5,"answer":2,"rationale":"Feeling that your actions make no difference is resignation. Its antidote is \"I'm not helpless. I can make a difference.\" A: Macho is trying to prove yourself. B: Anti-authority is resenting rules."},{"n":47,"stem":"In the DECIDE model, what comes right after Estimate?","options":["Detect","Choose","Evaluate"],"domain":5,"answer":1,"rationale":"DECIDE runs Detect, Estimate, Choose, Identify, Do, Evaluate. A: Detect comes first. C: Evaluate is last."},{"n":48,"stem":"When should you run the 5P check?","options":["Only the night before the job","Only after an emergency","Before launch and again at key points, such as midway or any time things change"],"domain":5,"answer":2,"rationale":"The 5P check is meant to be repeated at key decision points, since conditions change. A: One check the night before misses changes on the day. B: Waiting until an emergency defeats its purpose."},{"n":49,"stem":"How is chronic fatigue different from acute fatigue?","options":["Chronic fatigue goes away after one good night's sleep","Chronic fatigue builds up over a long time and needs a longer recovery","They are the same thing"],"domain":5,"answer":1,"rationale":"Acute fatigue is short-term and fixed by rest. Chronic fatigue builds over weeks and takes longer to recover from. A: That describes acute fatigue. C: They differ in cause and recovery."},{"n":50,"stem":"You want to take an over-the-counter cold medicine before a job. What should you do first?","options":["Take a double dose so it works faster","Read the label, and do not fly if it warns of drowsiness or impaired judgment","Nothing, because over-the-counter medicines are always safe for flying"],"domain":5,"answer":1,"rationale":"Many common medicines cause drowsiness. If the label warns of it, you are not fit to fly. A: A higher dose increases side effects. C: Over-the-counter does not mean safe for flight."},{"n":51,"stem":"Empty-field myopia happens when","options":["you stare at a featureless sky and your eyes relax to a short focus","you look at a bright screen for too long","you fly at night without lights"],"domain":5,"answer":0,"rationale":"With nothing to focus on, the eyes tend to focus a short distance away, so distant aircraft are missed. B: Screen glare is a different problem. C: Lighting is not the cause."},{"n":52,"stem":"Before every flight, what should you know about a lost control link?","options":["How the aircraft is programmed to respond, such as hover, land, or return home","Nothing, because lost link never happens on new drones","Only the manufacturer's phone number"],"domain":5,"answer":0,"rationale":"Knowing the programmed lost-link behavior lets you predict where the aircraft will go. B: Lost link can happen to any drone. C: A phone number does not tell you what the aircraft will do."},{"n":53,"stem":"Why should you avoid launching from a steel bridge deck?","options":["Steel reflects sunlight into the camera","The metal can disturb the compass and cause erratic flight","The deck is always too slippery"],"domain":5,"answer":1,"rationale":"Large metal structures can throw off the compass and cause the aircraft to drift or fly erratically. A: Glare is not the main concern. C: Footing may matter, but the compass is the flight hazard."},{"n":54,"stem":"What should you do with your flight logs after an emergency?","options":["Delete them to free up space","Post them online right away","Save them, because they help explain what happened"],"domain":5,"answer":2,"rationale":"Logs are evidence of what the aircraft did and can support any report or investigation. A: Deleting logs throws away the best record. B: Publishing them is not a safety step."},{"n":55,"stem":"What is the phonetic word for the letter X?","options":["Xylophone","X-ray","Xenon"],"domain":5,"answer":1,"rationale":"The aviation phonetic alphabet uses X-ray. A: Xylophone is not part of the alphabet. C: Xenon is not part of the alphabet."},{"n":56,"stem":"What is ATIS?","options":["A continuous recording of airport weather and information","The tower's voice frequency","An automated landing system"],"domain":5,"answer":0,"rationale":"ATIS is a recorded broadcast of current weather, runway in use, and other airport information. B: The tower frequency is separate. C: ATIS does not land aircraft."},{"n":57,"stem":"A runway is numbered 18. About what magnetic direction does it point?","options":["018 degrees","180 degrees","018 true degrees"],"domain":5,"answer":1,"rationale":"Runway numbers are the magnetic heading divided by 10, so 18 points about 180 degrees. A: Add a zero, do not add a leading zero. C: Runway numbers are magnetic, and the value is 180."},{"n":58,"stem":"What does a displaced threshold mean for crewed aircraft?","options":["The area before it may be used for takeoff, but not for landing","The runway is closed","The runway is for helicopters only"],"domain":5,"answer":0,"rationale":"Arrows lead to a displaced threshold. That area can be used for takeoff and rollout, but not for touchdown. B: A closed runway is marked with an X. C: Helipads have their own markings."},{"n":59,"stem":"During preflight, a motor feels gritty when you turn it by hand. What should you do?","options":["Fly gently to see if it clears","Ignore it, since motors often feel rough","Clean and inspect it, and do not fly until the problem is fixed"],"domain":5,"answer":2,"rationale":"A gritty motor may have debris or a failing bearing. Fix it before flight. A: Testing it in the air risks a failure. B: Roughness is a warning sign."},{"n":60,"stem":"Which maintenance record is the best habit for a drone operator?","options":["No record, since Part 107 never needs one","A log of flights, repairs, and battery cycles","Only the purchase receipt"],"domain":5,"answer":1,"rationale":"Logging flights, repairs, and battery use helps you spot trends and plan maintenance. A: Good records are a best practice, even when no specific form is required. C: A receipt says nothing about the aircraft's condition."}]},{"id":5,"name":"Practice Test 5","figure":{"label":"Figure 5.1","src":"images/f14_5.png","caption":"Fictional Excerpt C. Use this figure for the airspace items that refer to Figure 5.1. Not for navigation.","alt":"Fictional chart with Granite City International inside three solid blue rings labeled 100 over SFC, 100 over 25, and 100 over 40, plus Miller Field in a dashed blue ring labeled 33, obstacles labeled 1590 (702) and 1865 (998), a river, roads, and numbered points 1 through 5."},"questions":[{"n":1,"stem":"Your drone strikes a bystander, who faints for a few seconds and then feels fine and refuses medical care. Must you report this to the FAA?","options":["No, because the person refused medical care","Yes, because any loss of consciousness is reportable","No, because the person recovered within a minute"],"domain":1,"answer":1,"rationale":"Section 107.9 requires a report within 10 calendar days for serious injury or any loss of consciousness. A: Refusing care does not cancel the loss of consciousness. C: The rule says any loss of consciousness, however brief."},{"n":2,"stem":"Your drone destroys a garden statue worth $350. It cannot be repaired. Must you report the accident to the FAA?","options":["No, because the fair market value of the destroyed property is $500 or less","Yes, because all property damage is reportable","Yes, because a total loss is always reportable"],"domain":1,"answer":0,"rationale":"Property damage is not reportable when a total loss has a fair market value of $500 or less. B: Part 107 sets a $500 threshold, so not all damage counts. C: A total loss is judged by its fair market value, and $350 is under the line."},{"n":3,"stem":"Your $2,000 drone crashes in an empty field and is destroyed. Nobody is hurt, and nothing else is damaged. What must you report?","options":["The loss, because it exceeds $500","The loss, because any crash is reportable","Nothing, because damage to the drone itself does not count"],"domain":1,"answer":2,"rationale":"The property damage trigger covers property other than the small UA, so the drone's own value does not count. A: The $500 figure applies to other property. B: Only the events listed in section 107.9 are reportable."},{"n":4,"stem":"In Alaska, how is civil twilight defined for Part 107?","options":["The 30 minutes before sunrise and the 30 minutes after sunset","The hour before sunrise and the hour after sunset","By the Air Almanac"],"domain":1,"answer":2,"rationale":"Alaska uses the Air Almanac because the sun behaves differently at high latitudes. A: That definition applies everywhere except Alaska. B: No version of the rule uses one hour."},{"n":5,"stem":"Visual line of sight must let the crew do which of these?","options":["Know the aircraft's location, attitude, altitude, and direction of flight","Read the registration number on the aircraft","See the aircraft's battery level on the airframe"],"domain":1,"answer":0,"rationale":"Section 107.31 requires enough sight to know where the aircraft is and what it is doing, and to watch the airspace for hazards. B: You do not need to read markings in flight. C: Battery level comes from telemetry, not from sight."},{"n":6,"stem":"With no category of operations over people, whom may you fly directly over?","options":["Any adult who gives verbal permission","A person directly participating in the operation","Any person standing outdoors, if you keep moving"],"domain":1,"answer":1,"rationale":"Section 107.39 allows flight over people who are directly participating. A: Permission from a bystander does not make that person a participant. C: Staying in motion does not remove the rule."},{"n":7,"stem":"The Category 2 label on your drone has worn off. What must you do before flying over people?","options":["Fly anyway, since the Declaration of Compliance is still on file","Send the drone back to the manufacturer for a new airworthiness certificate","As remote PIC, label the aircraft again so that the label is legible and prominent"],"domain":1,"answer":2,"rationale":"Section 107.135 lets the remote PIC replace a damaged, destroyed, or missing label before flying over people. A: The aircraft must carry a label for this kind of flight. B: Category 2 does not use an airworthiness certificate."},{"n":8,"stem":"Your Category 2 drone will cross a busy road with moving cars, outside any closed site. How may it fly over those cars?","options":["Without sustained flight over them","By hovering above them, if you stay at 100 feet or higher","It may not fly over moving vehicles at all"],"domain":1,"answer":0,"rationale":"For Categories 1, 2, and 3, the aircraft must either stay in a closed site with notice or not maintain sustained flight over moving vehicles. B: Hovering is sustained flight, and altitude does not fix that. C: The rule allows it under these conditions."},{"n":9,"stem":"Without a category of operations, you may fly over a person who is","options":["walking in an open park","inside a stationary vehicle that can provide reasonable protection","riding in a moving car"],"domain":1,"answer":1,"rationale":"Section 107.39 allows flight over people under a covered structure or inside a stationary vehicle that can give reasonable protection. A: A person in an open park has no such protection. C: Moving vehicles have their own, stricter rule."},{"n":10,"stem":"Before flight, how much power must you confirm the small UAS has?","options":["Enough to reach maximum altitude","At least 75 percent battery charge","Enough to operate for the intended operational time"],"domain":1,"answer":2,"rationale":"Section 107.49 requires enough available power for the small UAS to operate for the intended operational time. A: The rule is tied to the planned flight, not to altitude. B: Part 107 sets no fixed percentage."},{"n":11,"stem":"During preflight, you notice that a payload is held by a loose strap. What does section 107.49 require?","options":["Securing it so that it will not adversely affect flight","Removing all payloads before any flight","Noting it in your flight log and flying as planned"],"domain":1,"answer":0,"rationale":"Before flight, you must make sure any object attached or carried is secure and does not adversely affect flight characteristics. B: Payloads are allowed. They must be secure. C: A log entry does not fix a loose load."},{"n":12,"stem":"Your drone is 1,800 feet horizontally from a cloud. Is this allowed under the Part 107 weather minimums?","options":["Yes, because it is more than 500 feet away","No, because the rule requires at least 2,000 feet horizontally","Yes, as long as visibility is at least 3 statute miles"],"domain":1,"answer":1,"rationale":"Section 107.51 requires at least 2,000 feet horizontally from clouds. A: 500 feet is the distance below clouds, not the horizontal distance. C: Visibility and cloud clearance are separate rules, and both must be met."},{"n":13,"stem":"You fly 500 feet horizontally from a 600-foot AGL tower. With no waiver, what is your highest allowed altitude?","options":["400 feet AGL","600 feet AGL","1,000 feet AGL"],"domain":1,"answer":0,"rationale":"The structure allowance applies only within a 400-foot radius of the structure. At 500 feet away, the normal 400-foot AGL limit applies. B: Matching the tower's height is not an allowance. C: This applies the allowance outside its 400-foot radius."},{"n":14,"stem":"Which form do you complete in IACRA to apply for a Remote Pilot Certificate?","options":["The AKTR","The Declaration of Compliance","FAA Form 8710-13"],"domain":1,"answer":2,"rationale":"FAA Form 8710-13 is the application completed in IACRA after you pass the test. A: The AKTR is your test report, not the application. B: A Declaration of Compliance relates to aircraft flown over people or to Remote ID."},{"n":15,"stem":"After you pass the knowledge test, about how long can it take for the result to appear in IACRA?","options":["Up to 10 minutes","Up to 48 hours","Up to 30 days"],"domain":1,"answer":1,"rationale":"Allow up to 48 hours for the test to appear in IACRA. A: Results do not reach IACRA that quickly. C: The wait is far shorter than a month."},{"n":16,"stem":"An FAA inspector asks to inspect your small UAS. What does section 107.7 require?","options":["Refuse unless the inspector has a court order","Allow it only if the aircraft has been in an accident","Allow the inspection or test on request"],"domain":1,"answer":2,"rationale":"Section 107.7 requires you to make the small UAS available for inspection or testing on request. A: The rule does not call for a court order. B: The duty does not depend on an accident."},{"n":17,"stem":"A 70-pound drone needs to be registered. Which system applies?","options":["The Part 47 aircraft registry","Part 107 registration through FAADroneZone","The recreational registration"],"domain":1,"answer":0,"rationale":"An aircraft of 55 pounds or more is outside Part 107 and is registered through the Part 47 aircraft registry. B: FAADroneZone Part 107 registration is for small unmanned aircraft. C: Recreational registration does not fit this aircraft."},{"n":18,"stem":"How can you check whether a drone model complies with Remote ID?","options":["Look for the word \"smart\" on the box","Search the FAA's Declaration of Compliance site","Call the nearest control tower"],"domain":1,"answer":1,"rationale":"The FAA's Declaration of Compliance site lists Remote ID compliance. A: Packaging words prove nothing. C: Towers do not keep this information."},{"n":19,"stem":"You add a Remote ID broadcast module to your registered drone. What must you do with your registration?","options":["Nothing, because modules are not tracked","Register the drone again under a new number","Add the module's serial number to the aircraft's registration"],"domain":1,"answer":2,"rationale":"The module's serial number goes on the registration for the aircraft it is used with. A: The FAA needs the serial number to link the broadcast to the aircraft. B: You do not need a new registration number."},{"n":20,"stem":"How long does FAA approval of a FRIA last?","options":["48 calendar months","24 calendar months","Indefinitely"],"domain":1,"answer":0,"rationale":"An approved FRIA is in effect for 48 calendar months and must then be renewed. B: 24 calendar months is the recency period for remote pilots. C: A FRIA expires unless it is renewed."},{"n":21,"stem":"What must a waiver application show?","options":["That the pilot has at least 100 logged flights","That the operation can be safely conducted under the terms of the waiver","That no other pilot has applied for the same area"],"domain":1,"answer":1,"rationale":"The FAA issues a waiver when the applicant shows the operation can be done safely under its terms. A: Part 107 sets no fixed flight count. C: Other applicants have no bearing on your waiver."},{"n":22,"stem":"You are paid to carry a client's package by drone under Part 107. Which limit applies?","options":["The package must weigh less than 5 pounds","The flight may take place only in Class G airspace","The flight must stay within one state"],"domain":1,"answer":2,"rationale":"Paid carriage is allowed in a narrow form: under 55 pounds in total, within visual line of sight, within one state, with no hazardous material, and not from a moving vehicle. A: There is no 5-pound limit. The 55-pound total is what counts. B: Airspace access is handled by the usual authorization rules."},{"n":23,"stem":"You decide to give up your Remote Pilot Certificate for good. How do you surrender it?","options":["Send the FAA a signed statement asking that it be cancelled","Mail it to your testing center","Simply stop taking recurrent training"],"domain":1,"answer":0,"rationale":"A holder may surrender a certificate for cancellation by sending a signed statement to the FAA. B: Testing centers do not handle certificates. C: Letting recency lapse is not a surrender. The certificate would still exist."},{"n":24,"stem":"When must a remote PIC be designated?","options":["Only when a client is on site","Before or during the flight","The day before the flight"],"domain":1,"answer":1,"rationale":"Section 107.19 says a remote PIC must be designated before or during the flight. A: The rule applies to every flight, with or without a client. C: No advance notice is needed."},{"n":25,"stem":"Which identification must you bring to the testing center?","options":["Your drone's registration certificate","A credit card with your name on it","ID showing your photo, date of birth, signature, and physical residential address"],"domain":1,"answer":2,"rationale":"The testing center requires identification showing your photo, date of birth, signature, and physical residential address. A: Drone registration does not identify you. B: A credit card does not show the required details."},{"n":26,"stem":"Who provides the free online recurrent training for remote pilots?","options":["The FAA Safety Team (FAASTeam)","PSI testing centers","Local law enforcement"],"domain":1,"answer":0,"rationale":"The ALC-677 recurrent course is free and online through the FAASTeam. B: PSI delivers the knowledge test, not recurrent training. C: Police have no role in pilot training."},{"n":27,"stem":"You wear contact lenses to see the drone. Does this meet the visual line of sight rule?","options":["No, because vision must be fully unaided","Yes, because corrective lenses are allowed","Only with a waiver"],"domain":1,"answer":1,"rationale":"Section 107.31 allows corrective lenses, such as glasses or contact lenses. A: Corrective lenses are the stated exception. C: No waiver is needed for corrective lenses."},{"n":28,"stem":"In Ohio, sunrise is at 6:40 a.m. You take off at 6:20 a.m. with no anti-collision light. Is this allowed?","options":["Yes, because the sun is almost up","Yes, if the drone has a white position light","No, because this is civil twilight, which requires anti-collision lighting"],"domain":1,"answer":2,"rationale":"6:20 a.m. falls in the 30 minutes before sunrise, so the anti-collision lighting rule applies. A: Near sunrise is still twilight until the sun is up. B: A position light does not replace anti-collision lighting."},{"n":29,"stem":"A small UA may pass over, under, or ahead of a crewed aircraft only when it is","options":["well clear","higher than the aircraft","in contact with ATC"],"domain":1,"answer":0,"rationale":"Section 107.37 says a small UA may not pass over, under, or ahead of another aircraft unless it is well clear. B: Being higher gives no right to cross. C: Radio contact does not change the rule."},{"n":30,"stem":"What does a dashed magenta line around an airport mean?","options":["Class D","Class E that begins at the surface, so authorization is needed","Class E that begins at 700 feet AGL"],"domain":2,"answer":1,"rationale":"A dashed magenta line marks Class E to the surface, which is covered by section 107.41. A: Class D is a dashed blue line. C: A 700-foot floor is shown by a faded magenta band."},{"n":31,"stem":"How far ahead can you request a LAANC authorization?","options":["Up to 7 days","Up to 30 days","Up to 90 days"],"domain":2,"answer":2,"rationale":"LAANC requests can be made up to 90 days in advance. A: The window is longer than a week. B: The window is longer than a month."},{"n":32,"stem":"What can B4UFLY do for a pilot in controlled airspace?","options":["Show airspace information, but not issue an authorization","Issue an authorization instantly","Replace the need to check NOTAMs for TFRs"],"domain":2,"answer":0,"rationale":"B4UFLY gives information only. It does not issue authorizations. B: Authorization comes through LAANC or FAADroneZone. C: You must still check for TFRs before flight."},{"n":33,"stem":"A UAS Facility Map grid shows 200, and you need to fly at 250 feet AGL. What is your path?","options":["LAANC can approve up to 400 feet anywhere in the grid","Request further coordination for the higher altitude","You cannot fly there at all"],"domain":2,"answer":1,"rationale":"LAANC approves automatically up to the grid altitude. Further coordination can be requested for higher altitudes, up to 400 feet. A: Automatic approval stops at the grid altitude. C: The grid value is not a ban on higher requests."},{"n":34,"stem":"A wildfire has a TFR in place. Does it apply to your drone?","options":["No, TFRs apply only to crewed aircraft","Only if you are being paid","Yes, you must comply with it"],"domain":2,"answer":2,"rationale":"Section 107.47 requires compliance with TFRs, including those over disaster areas such as wildfires. A: TFRs apply to drones. B: The TFR applies whether or not you are paid."},{"n":35,"stem":"What is the role of a UAS Facility Map?","options":["It gives information on altitudes that may be approved, but it does not authorize a flight","It authorizes flight at the altitudes it shows","It replaces the sectional chart for all purposes"],"domain":2,"answer":0,"rationale":"Facility maps are for information. You still need an authorization. B: The map itself gives no permission. C: A sectional chart shows much more than a grid of altitudes."},{"n":36,"stem":"A Class D tower is part-time and is now closed. Where do you find what the airspace becomes?","options":["The airport's website","The nearest METAR","The Chart Supplement"],"domain":2,"answer":2,"rationale":"The Chart Supplement entry tells you whether the airspace becomes Class E or Class G after hours. A: A website is not the source for the airspace. B: A METAR reports weather, not airspace."},{"n":37,"stem":"(Refer to Figure 5.1.) How high does the Class B reach above Granite City International?","options":["10,000 feet","9,305 feet","10,695 feet"],"domain":2,"fig":true,"answer":1,"rationale":"The top is 10,000 feet MSL and the field elevation is 695 feet: 10,000 - 695 = 9,305 feet above the airport. A: 10,000 feet is the ceiling in MSL, not its height above the airport. C: This adds the elevation instead of subtracting it."},{"n":38,"stem":"(Refer to Figure 5.1.) Which frequency is the UNICOM at Miller Field?","options":["122.7","125.15","120.3"],"domain":2,"fig":true,"answer":0,"rationale":"The last item in the second line of the data block is the UNICOM: 122.7. B: 125.15 is Miller's control tower. C: 120.3 is the Granite City tower."},{"n":39,"stem":"(Refer to Figure 5.1.) You inspect the obstacle labeled 1865 (998), staying 200 feet above its top and within 400 feet of it. Are you in Class B?","options":["Yes, because you are inside the outer ring","Yes, because the floor there is 2,500 feet MSL","No, because you are at 2,065 feet MSL and the floor there is 4,000 feet MSL"],"domain":2,"fig":true,"answer":2,"rationale":"The obstacle is between the middle and outer rings, where the floor is 4,000 feet MSL. 1,865 + 200 = 2,065 feet MSL, which is below the floor. A: Being inside a ring does not put you in Class B until you reach its floor. B: 2,500 feet MSL is the floor of the middle ring, and you would still be below it."},{"n":40,"stem":"(Refer to Figure 5.1.) At Point 2, the ground is about 700 feet MSL. How far above the ground is the Class B floor?","options":["1,800 feet","2,500 feet","3,300 feet"],"domain":2,"fig":true,"answer":0,"rationale":"The middle ring's floor is 2,500 feet MSL: 2,500 - 700 = 1,800 feet above the ground. B: 2,500 feet is the floor in MSL, not its height above the ground. C: This adds the two numbers."},{"n":41,"stem":"(Refer to Figure 5.1.) Which numbered point needs a Class B authorization for a flight at 200 feet AGL?","options":["Point 3","Point 1","Point 4"],"domain":2,"fig":true,"answer":1,"rationale":"Point 1 is inside the inner ring, where the Class B starts at the surface. A: Point 3 is under the outer ring's 4,000-foot floor. C: Point 4 is outside all three rings."},{"n":42,"stem":"A METAR shows 18012G22KT. What does this mean?","options":["Wind from 120 degrees at 18 knots","Wind from 180 degrees at 12 knots, gusting to 22 knots","Wind from 180 degrees, with gusts every 12 to 22 minutes"],"domain":3,"answer":1,"rationale":"The first three digits give the direction, the next two give the speed, and G gives the gust speed. A: This reverses the direction and speed. C: The numbers are speeds in knots, not times."},{"n":43,"stem":"What does a Convective SIGMET warn about?","options":["Mountain obscuration","Frost on parked aircraft","Thunderstorm hazards, such as lines of storms, tornadoes, and large hail"],"domain":3,"answer":2,"rationale":"A Convective SIGMET covers thunderstorm hazards and is valid for up to 2 hours. A: Mountain obscuration belongs to AIRMET Sierra. B: A Convective SIGMET does not deal with frost."},{"n":44,"stem":"On a clear, calm morning, what does a surface temperature inversion often do?","options":["Traps smoke and haze near the ground, which can reduce visibility","Creates towering clouds and heavy showers","Raises the cloud base above 10,000 feet"],"domain":3,"answer":0,"rationale":"An inversion acts as a lid that traps smoke, haze, and fog below it. B: Those are signs of unstable air, and an inversion is very stable. C: An inversion does not work that way."},{"n":45,"stem":"You add a heavier camera to a multirotor. What is the usual effect?","options":["Longer flight time, since the extra weight adds stability","No change, since the flight controller cancels out the extra weight","Shorter flight time, since the motors must work harder"],"domain":4,"answer":2,"rationale":"Extra weight means more power to stay in the air, which shortens endurance and reduces your reserve. A: Extra weight does not extend flight time. B: The controller compensates, but it uses battery and margin to do so."},{"n":46,"stem":"A pilot launches the moment the client arrives, skipping the preflight to \"get on with it.\" Which hazardous attitude is this?","options":["Impulsivity","Invulnerability","Resignation"],"domain":5,"answer":0,"rationale":"Doing the first thing that comes to mind is impulsivity. Its antidote is \"Not so fast. Think first.\" B: Invulnerability is believing an accident cannot happen to you. C: Resignation is believing nothing you do matters."},{"n":47,"stem":"Which list gives the five items of the 5P check?","options":["Pilot, Payload, Power, Propellers, Permission","Plan, Plane, Pilot, Passengers, Programming","Preflight, Position, Path, Power, Parking"],"domain":5,"answer":1,"rationale":"The five Ps are Plan, Plane, Pilot, Passengers, and Programming. A: These are useful ideas, but they are not the 5P model. C: This is not the 5P model."},{"n":48,"stem":"What are the three steps of the 3P model?","options":["Plan, Prepare, Proceed","Pilot, Plane, Programming","Perceive, Process, Perform"],"domain":5,"answer":2,"rationale":"The 3P model is Perceive, Process, and Perform. A: These words sound right, but they are not the model. B: These are items from the 5P check."},{"n":49,"stem":"While you stare at your drone's light at night, it seems to wander on its own. What is this illusion?","options":["Empty-field myopia","Autokinesis","Hypoxia"],"domain":5,"answer":1,"rationale":"Autokinesis is the apparent motion of a stationary light in the dark. Keep your eyes moving and compare the light to a fixed reference. A: Empty-field myopia is a focus problem in a featureless sky. C: Hypoxia is a lack of oxygen, not a visual illusion."},{"n":50,"stem":"Which light is best for reading a checklist during a night flight?","options":["A phone flashlight at full brightness","A dim red light","A bright white headlamp"],"domain":5,"answer":1,"rationale":"A dim red light helps protect your dark adaptation. A: Bright light can undo your night vision in seconds. C: Bright white light has the same problem."},{"n":51,"stem":"What is the safest way to charge a lithium drone battery?","options":["On a non-flammable surface, with the correct charger, and attended","Overnight on a bed so that it stays warm","In a closed car, to keep it away from people"],"domain":5,"answer":0,"rationale":"Charging is one of the most likely times for a fire, so use the correct charger on a non-flammable surface and stay with it. B: Never charge overnight or on anything that can burn. C: A closed car puts the pack near fuel and fabric, and you cannot watch it."},{"n":52,"stem":"How should you store lithium drone batteries between jobs?","options":["Fully charged, in direct sunlight","Fully drained, in a hot garage","At about half charge, somewhere cool and dry, or as the manufacturer directs"],"domain":5,"answer":2,"rationale":"About half charge in a cool, dry place is the usual advice, and the manufacturer's instructions come first. A: Heat and a full charge both stress the pack. B: Heat and full discharge can damage the pack."},{"n":53,"stem":"A controller says something to you and you reply \"Wilco.\" What does this mean?","options":["I have received your message, but I may not comply","I will comply","Please say that again"],"domain":5,"answer":1,"rationale":"Wilco means \"I will comply.\" A: Roger confirms receipt, and it does not promise compliance. C: That phrase is \"say again.\""},{"n":54,"stem":"Which leg of the traffic pattern runs parallel to the runway, opposite to the landing direction?","options":["Downwind","Base","Upwind"],"domain":5,"answer":0,"rationale":"The downwind leg runs parallel to the runway in the direction opposite to landing. B: The base leg runs at a right angle to the runway. C: The upwind leg runs in the landing direction."},{"n":55,"stem":"A controller reports \"traffic two o'clock.\" Where is the traffic?","options":["Behind and to the left","Directly behind","Ahead and to the right"],"domain":5,"answer":2,"rationale":"The nose is 12 o'clock and the right wing is 3 o'clock, so two o'clock is ahead and to the right. A: That would be about seven or eight o'clock. B: That would be six o'clock."},{"n":56,"stem":"A part-time tower has closed for the night. Which frequency should you listen to for traffic?","options":["The ATIS","The CTAF","The emergency frequency"],"domain":5,"answer":1,"rationale":"When the tower is closed, pilots announce their positions on the CTAF. A: ATIS is a recording, not a traffic frequency. C: Emergency frequencies are not used for routine position calls."},{"n":57,"stem":"You climb through 200 feet on a calm morning, and the aircraft suddenly lurches. What is the likely cause?","options":["Wind shear at the top of a surface inversion","A low battery","The compass needing a calibration update"],"domain":5,"answer":0,"rationale":"A surface inversion can leave still air near the ground under a strong wind just above it. B: A low battery does not cause a sudden lurch at a set height. C: A compass problem is not tied to climbing through a layer."},{"n":58,"stem":"Near a runway, where are airplanes most likely to be at your altitude?","options":["Beside the middle of the runway","Off both ends of the runway","Under the downwind leg"],"domain":5,"answer":1,"rationale":"Arriving aircraft descend on base and final, and departing aircraft climb off the other end. A: The sides of the runway are safer than the ends. C: Traffic on downwind is usually well above 400 feet."},{"n":59,"stem":"Your battery is too low for the aircraft to reach home. What should you do?","options":["Push on, and hope it makes it","Climb to save power","Land at the closest safe spot"],"domain":5,"answer":2,"rationale":"If the aircraft cannot reach home, land at the closest safe spot and walk out to get it. A: Trying to stretch the battery risks a forced landing somewhere unsafe. B: Climbing uses more power."},{"n":60,"stem":"About what altitude do you expect a typical airplane traffic pattern to be?","options":["1,000 feet above the ground","400 feet above the ground","3,000 feet above the ground"],"domain":5,"answer":0,"rationale":"Traffic patterns are commonly flown at about 1,000 feet above the ground. B: That is the Part 107 altitude limit, not pattern altitude. C: This is much higher than a typical pattern."}]}],"decks":[{"name":"Rules and Numbers","cards":[{"front":"Small UA weight limit","back":"Less than 55 pounds at takeoff, counting everything on board or attached."},{"front":"Maximum groundspeed","back":"87 knots (100 miles per hour)."},{"front":"Maximum altitude","back":"400 feet AGL. Within a 400-foot radius of a structure, up to 400 feet above its top."},{"front":"Minimum flight visibility","back":"3 statute miles, measured from the control station."},{"front":"Cloud clearance","back":"At least 500 feet below clouds and 2,000 feet horizontally from them."},{"front":"Accident report deadline","back":"Within 10 calendar days, through FAADroneZone."},{"front":"What makes an accident reportable?","back":"Serious injury, any loss of consciousness, or damage to other property unless repair is $500 or less, or a total loss is worth $500 or less."},{"front":"Does damage to the drone itself count?","back":"No. The property trigger covers property other than the small UA."},{"front":"Alcohol: hours from drinking to flying","back":"8 hours."},{"front":"Alcohol: blood alcohol limit","back":"Below 0.04, and you may not fly while under the influence at all."},{"front":"Minimum age to hold the certificate","back":"16."},{"front":"Minimum age to take the knowledge test","back":"14."},{"front":"Knowledge test format","back":"60 scored questions, 3 options each, 120 minutes, 70 percent (42 correct) to pass."},{"front":"Wait after failing the test","back":"14 days."},{"front":"How long a passing test result stays valid","back":"24 calendar months for applying."},{"front":"Temporary certificate","back":"Valid for up to 120 calendar days."},{"front":"Recurrent training interval","back":"Every 24 calendar months, ending on the last day of the month."},{"front":"ALC-677","back":"The free online recurrent course for remote pilots."},{"front":"ALC-515","back":"The recurrent course for Part 61 pilots with a current flight review."},{"front":"Does the certificate expire?","back":"No. Recency lapses, and recurrent training restores it."},{"front":"Address change deadline","back":"30 days."},{"front":"Application form","back":"FAA Form 8710-13, completed in IACRA."},{"front":"Part 107 registration","back":"Each drone separately, $5, valid for 3 years, through FAADroneZone."},{"front":"Recreational registration","back":"One number for all drones you own. Drones of 0.55 pounds or less are exempt."},{"front":"Who may ask for your certificate?","back":"The FAA, NTSB, TSA, and any federal, state, or local law enforcement officer."},{"front":"Who may fly without a certificate?","back":"A person under the direct supervision of a remote PIC who can take over immediately."},{"front":"Who has final authority over a flight?","back":"The remote PIC."},{"front":"Emergency deviation","back":"You may deviate only as far as the emergency requires, and send a written report if the FAA asks."},{"front":"Night flight needs","back":"Current training or test after April 6, 2021, and anti-collision lighting visible for 3 statute miles."},{"front":"Civil twilight (outside Alaska)","back":"The 30 minutes before sunrise and the 30 minutes after sunset. Lighting is required."},{"front":"Visual observer","back":"Optional. Needs no certificate. Must keep effective communication with the crew."},{"front":"How many aircraft at once?","back":"One, unless you have a waiver."},{"front":"Hazardous material","back":"Prohibited, and the rule cannot be waived."},{"front":"Dropping objects","back":"Allowed if it creates no undue hazard to people or property."},{"front":"Flying from a moving vehicle","back":"Never from an aircraft. From land or water vehicles only over a sparsely populated area, and not carrying others' property for hire."},{"front":"Right of way","back":"The small UA yields to all aircraft and may pass only when well clear."},{"front":"Paid cargo limits","back":"Under 55 pounds in total, within visual line of sight, within one state, no hazardous material, not from a moving vehicle."},{"front":"Category 1","back":"0.55 pounds or less, with no exposed rotating parts that could lacerate skin."},{"front":"Category 2","back":"Declaration of Compliance and a label. Injury limit of 11 foot-pounds."},{"front":"Category 3","back":"Declaration of Compliance and a label. Injury limit of 25 foot-pounds. No open-air assemblies."},{"front":"Category 4","back":"Airworthiness certificate under Part 21, and flight manual limits."},{"front":"Remote ID paths","back":"Standard Remote ID, a broadcast module, or flying inside a FRIA."},{"front":"Broadcast stops in flight","back":"Land as soon as practicable."},{"front":"Who can apply for a FRIA?","back":"FAA-recognized community-based organizations and educational institutions."},{"front":"Drug or alcohol conviction","back":"Denial of an application for up to 1 year, or suspension or revocation of the certificate."},{"front":"Waivable rule examples","back":"Night, visual line of sight, visual observer, one aircraft, right of way (107.37(a)), over people, airspace, operating limits."},{"front":"Rules that cannot be waived","back":"Examples are hazardous material (107.36), alcohol and drugs (107.27), and accident reporting (107.9)."}]},{"name":"Airspace and Charts","cards":[{"front":"Authorization is needed in","back":"Class B, Class C, Class D, and the surface area of Class E designated for an airport."},{"front":"Airspace with no authorization needed","back":"Class G and other Class E."},{"front":"Class A begins at","back":"18,000 feet MSL."},{"front":"Typical Class B ceiling","back":"About 10,000 feet MSL."},{"front":"Class B on a chart","back":"Solid blue rings, like an upside-down wedding cake."},{"front":"Class C on a chart","back":"Solid magenta rings."},{"front":"Class D on a chart","back":"A dashed blue circle, with the ceiling in a dashed box."},{"front":"Class E to the surface on a chart","back":"A dashed magenta line."},{"front":"Faded magenta band","back":"Class E begins at 700 feet AGL."},{"front":"Faded blue band","back":"Class E begins at 1,200 feet AGL."},{"front":"Class E with no other floor shown","back":"Begins at 14,500 feet MSL."},{"front":"Class D typical ceiling","back":"About 2,500 feet above the airport."},{"front":"Class C typical shape","back":"Inner core to about 5 nautical miles from the surface. Outer shelf to about 10 nautical miles from about 1,200 feet above the airport."},{"front":"Fraction 100 over 25","back":"Ceiling 10,000 feet MSL, floor 2,500 feet MSL."},{"front":"SFC in a fraction","back":"The floor is the surface."},{"front":"Dashed box with 33","back":"Class D ceiling of 3,300 feet MSL."},{"front":"Obstacle label 1865 (998)","back":"The top is 1,865 feet MSL. The height is 998 feet AGL."},{"front":"Ground elevation from an obstacle label","back":"Subtract the parentheses from the bold number."},{"front":"Airport data block: CT","back":"Control tower, followed by its frequency."},{"front":"Star or asterisk after a tower frequency","back":"A part-time tower."},{"front":"Circled C (plain C in the book)","back":"CTAF, the Common Traffic Advisory Frequency."},{"front":"First number on the airport data line","back":"Field elevation in feet MSL."},{"front":"L in an airport data line","back":"Runway lighting. An asterisk after the L means pilot-controlled or limited."},{"front":"72 in an airport data line","back":"Longest runway of 7,200 feet."},{"front":"Chart scale and cycle","back":"Sectional scale is 1:500,000. Revised on a 56-day cycle."},{"front":"Tower closed at Class D","back":"The airspace may become Class E or Class G. Check the Chart Supplement."},{"front":"Restricted area","back":"Get permission from the using or controlling agency."},{"front":"Prohibited area","back":"Closed for security or national welfare reasons. No flight without permission from the using or controlling agency, and do not expect to get it."},{"front":"Military Operations Area","back":"Military training. No permission needed, but use caution."},{"front":"Alert area","back":"High volume of pilot training or unusual activity. Be alert."},{"front":"Warning area","back":"Beyond 3 nautical miles from the coast, with possibly hazardous activity."},{"front":"National Security Area","back":"Voluntary avoidance, which a NOTAM can make temporarily prohibited."},{"front":"Military training route numbers","back":"Four digits: at or below 1,500 feet AGL. Three digits: goes above 1,500 feet AGL."},{"front":"Stadium restriction","back":"Within 3 nautical miles and up to 3,000 feet AGL, from 1 hour before to 1 hour after the event."},{"front":"LAANC","back":"Near-real-time authorization up to the grid altitude, requested up to 90 days ahead."},{"front":"LAANC grid shows 0","back":"No automatic approval. Request through FAADroneZone."},{"front":"B4UFLY","back":"Information only. It does not issue authorizations."}]},{"name":"METAR and TAF Codes","cards":[{"front":"METAR","back":"A routine weather report, normally once an hour."},{"front":"SPECI","back":"A special weather report issued between routine reports."},{"front":"TAF","back":"A terminal aerodrome forecast."},{"front":"Z in a time","back":"Zulu, or UTC."},{"front":"18012G22KT","back":"Wind from 180 degrees at 12 knots, gusting to 22 knots."},{"front":"VRB","back":"Variable wind direction."},{"front":"10SM","back":"Visibility of 10 statute miles."},{"front":"P6SM","back":"Visibility of more than 6 statute miles, in a TAF."},{"front":"FEW","back":"1 to 2 eighths of the sky covered."},{"front":"SCT","back":"3 to 4 eighths of the sky covered."},{"front":"BKN","back":"5 to 7 eighths of the sky covered. Counts as a ceiling."},{"front":"OVC","back":"8 eighths of the sky covered. Counts as a ceiling."},{"front":"BKN012","back":"A broken layer at 1,200 feet AGL."},{"front":"VV004","back":"Sky obscured, with a vertical visibility of 400 feet. Counts as a ceiling."},{"front":"CB after a cloud layer","back":"Cumulonimbus, a thunderstorm cloud."},{"front":"RA","back":"Rain."},{"front":"SN","back":"Snow."},{"front":"BR","back":"Mist."},{"front":"FG","back":"Fog."},{"front":"TS","back":"Thunderstorm."},{"front":"- before a weather code","back":"Light."},{"front":"+ before a weather code","back":"Heavy."},{"front":"VC","back":"In the vicinity."},{"front":"SH","back":"Showers."},{"front":"13/12","back":"Temperature of 13°C and dew point of 12°C."},{"front":"A3002","back":"Altimeter setting of 30.02 inches of mercury."},{"front":"RMK","back":"The remarks section begins."},{"front":"FM061800","back":"From the 6th at 1800Z, with the forecast changing from then on."},{"front":"TEMPO","back":"A temporary change within the stated period."},{"front":"BECMG","back":"A gradual change over a stated period."},{"front":"PROB30","back":"A 30 percent probability in the stated period."},{"front":"AIRMET Sierra","back":"IFR conditions and mountain obscuration."},{"front":"AIRMET Tango","back":"Turbulence, strong surface wind, and low-level wind shear."},{"front":"AIRMET Zulu","back":"Icing and freezing level."},{"front":"Convective SIGMET","back":"Thunderstorm hazards, valid for up to 2 hours."}]},{"name":"Airport and Radio","cards":[{"front":"CTAF","back":"The shared frequency at a non-towered airport, or at a towered one when the tower is closed."},{"front":"UNICOM","back":"The airport's non-government advisory radio."},{"front":"ATIS","back":"A continuous recording of airport weather and information."},{"front":"Standard traffic pattern turns","back":"Left, unless shown otherwise."},{"front":"Downwind leg","back":"Parallel to the runway, opposite to the landing direction."},{"front":"Base leg","back":"At a right angle to the runway."},{"front":"Typical pattern altitude","back":"About 1,000 feet above the ground."},{"front":"Danger zones near a runway","back":"Off both ends, where arriving and departing aircraft are lowest."},{"front":"Runway number 18","back":"About 180 degrees magnetic."},{"front":"Runway marking color","back":"White."},{"front":"X on a runway or taxiway","back":"Closed."},{"front":"Displaced threshold","back":"Usable for takeoff and rollout, but not for touchdown."},{"front":"Wind cone","back":"Shows wind direction."},{"front":"Traffic pattern indicators","back":"Show which way aircraft turn in the pattern."},{"front":"White and green beacon","back":"A civilian land airport."},{"front":"White and yellow beacon","back":"A water airport."},{"front":"Two quick white flashes between green","back":"A military airport."},{"front":"Roger","back":"\"I received your message.\""},{"front":"Wilco","back":"\"I will comply.\""},{"front":"Traffic two o'clock","back":"Ahead and to the right."},{"front":"The number 9 on the radio","back":"Niner."},{"front":"Phonetic alphabet: X","back":"X-ray."}]},{"name":"Mnemonics and Human Factors","cards":[{"front":"IMSAFE","back":"Illness, Medication, Stress, Alcohol, Fatigue, Emotion."},{"front":"PAVE","back":"Pilot, Aircraft, enVironment, External pressures."},{"front":"DECIDE","back":"Detect, Estimate, Choose, Identify, Do, Evaluate."},{"front":"5P check","back":"Plan, Plane, Pilot, Passengers, Programming."},{"front":"3P model","back":"Perceive, Process, Perform."},{"front":"Anti-authority antidote","back":"\"Follow the rules. They are usually right.\""},{"front":"Impulsivity antidote","back":"\"Not so fast. Think first.\""},{"front":"Invulnerability antidote","back":"\"It could happen to me.\""},{"front":"Macho antidote","back":"\"Taking chances is foolish.\""},{"front":"Resignation antidote","back":"\"I'm not helpless. I can make a difference.\""},{"front":"Dark adaptation","back":"About 30 minutes for the eyes to adjust fully."},{"front":"Best way to see at night","back":"Look a little to one side of the object."},{"front":"Autokinesis","back":"A stationary light seems to move when you stare at it in the dark."},{"front":"Empty-field myopia","back":"In a featureless sky, your eyes relax to a short focus."},{"front":"Tingling and dizziness under stress","back":"Hyperventilation."},{"front":"Acute and chronic fatigue","back":"Acute is short-term and fixed by rest. Chronic builds up and takes longer to recover from."},{"front":"Headache and tiredness in the heat","back":"Dehydration. Stop, drink water, and find shade."},{"front":"CRM closed-loop communication","back":"The message is repeated back and confirmed."}]},{"name":"Loading and Performance","cards":[{"front":"Load factor at 30 degrees of bank","back":"1.15."},{"front":"Load factor at 45 degrees of bank","back":"1.41."},{"front":"Load factor at 60 degrees of bank","back":"2.0."},{"front":"Standard atmosphere at sea level","back":"29.92 inHg and 15°C (59°F)."},{"front":"Standard lapse rate","back":"About 2°C per 1,000 feet."},{"front":"Three H's of high density altitude","back":"High, hot, and humid."},{"front":"Effect of high density altitude","back":"Thinner air and reduced performance."},{"front":"Is humid air heavier?","back":"No. Water vapor is lighter than dry air, so humid air is less dense."},{"front":"Cloud base estimate","back":"Temperature and dew point spread in °F, divided by 4.4, times 1,000 feet."},{"front":"Stall speed in a turn","back":"Rises with the square root of the load factor."},{"front":"Forward center of gravity on a multirotor","back":"The heavy-side motors work harder, which costs endurance and margin."},{"front":"Lithium battery storage","back":"About half charge, cool and dry, or as the manufacturer directs."}]}]};
